=== Dangling Else Grammar (ambiguous) ===
Productions:
  [0] S -> if E then S
  [1] S -> if E then S else S
  [2] S -> other
  [3] E -> cond

Configuration demonstrating the conflict:
  Stack: ... if E then S
  Input: else ...

  Option 1: REDUCE using [S -> if E then S]
            => The 'else' will attach to an outer 'if' (if one exists)

  Option 2: SHIFT 'else'
            => The 'else' will attach to the INNER 'if' (nearest-if rule)

  Convention: shift wins (YACC/Bison default) = nearest 'if' binding.
  This is the RIGHT behavior for most languages but requires a CHOICE,
  meaning the grammar is AMBIGUOUS.

=== Reduce-Reduce Conflict Grammar ===
Productions:
  [0] S -> A c
  [1] S -> B c
  [2] A -> a b
  [3] B -> a b

Configuration demonstrating the conflict:
  Stack: ... a b
  Input: c ...

  Option 1: REDUCE using [A -> a b]
  Option 2: REDUCE using [B -> a b]

  The parser cannot distinguish A from B using only 'a b' on the stack!
  This grammar is ambiguous; A and B are indistinguishable.
  Fix: merge A and B into a single nonterminal, or change their RHS.

=== Precedence as Conflict Resolution ===

Grammar:  E -> E + E | E * E | num   (ambiguous)

Config 1:  Stack: E + E   Input: + ...
  Conflict: reduce [E -> E + E]  OR  shift '+'
  Resolution via precedence: both '+' have equal precedence, LEFT assoc
  => REDUCE (left associativity: 1+2+3 = (1+2)+3)

Config 2:  Stack: E + E   Input: * ...
  Conflict: reduce [E -> E + E]  OR  shift '*'
  Resolution via precedence: '*' has higher precedence than '+'
  => SHIFT (let '*' bind more tightly: 1+2*3 = 1+(2*3))

Declared precedence rules in yacc/bison translate directly into
conflict-resolution entries in the parse table, replacing ambiguity
with a documented, predictable choice.

=== Summary of Conflict Types ===
Type               Cause                                    Example                        Fix
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Shift-Reduce       Can extend OR close the current phrase   Dangling else, operator precedence Declare precedence/associativity, or restructure grammar
Reduce-Reduce      Two completed productions match the stack top Indistinguishable nonterminals, over-general grammar Merge nonterminals, add distinguishing tokens, use LR(1)
